    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d737">

                <pb facs="0780" n="768" sameAs="frus:frus1942v05/pg768"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.796/773: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-02-04T20:00:00-03:00">February 4, 1942—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 10:18 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>348. Department’s 236, January 29<anchor n="82" xml:id="d737-f1"/> and my 342,
                    February 4.<anchor n="83" xml:id="d737-f2"/> Salgado says he will requisition
                    the planes at once and will appoint a commission to evaluate. He will turn the
                    planes over to us as soon as we agree on price and will give us legal title
                    thereto.</p>
                <p>He says there are seven planes scattered at Rio de Janeiro, Pernambuco and Natal.
                    He will furnish us with specifications, et cetera, as soon as the commission
                    gets underway.</p>
                <p>Salgado has just returned from São Paulo and gave me this information over the
                    telephone. He insisted on getting a commitment that we would buy if he
                    requisitioned (at a reasonable price of course).</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d737-f1" n="82" xml:id="d737-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; this telegram conveyed the interest of the United States military
                    authorities in obtaining a number of planes of the Italian Transcontinental Air
                    Lines, whose operations had been curtailed (832.796/759).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d737-f2" n="83" xml:id="d737-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that the Fiat-Argentina firm was also interested in
                    obtaining the equipment of the Italian company, but the Bank of Brazil rejected
                    the offer (832.796/769).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
